1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,360
This car has a certificate
that says it's allowed to fly.

2
00:00:04,043 --> 00:00:06,323
Slovak aviation authorities issued it

3
00:00:06,553 --> 00:00:09,603
in 2022, after seventy hours in the air

4
00:00:09,833 --> 00:00:12,372
and more than two hundred take-offs.

5
00:00:12,672 --> 00:00:14,112
And it's not the first.

6
00:00:14,532 --> 00:00:19,352
In 1956, the American government
certified a car that could fly.

7
00:00:20,066 --> 00:00:21,126
Six were built.

8
00:00:21,206 --> 00:00:24,586
Not enough people wanted one,
and it never went into production.

9
00:00:25,410 --> 00:00:27,230
We were promised flying cars.

10
00:00:27,910 --> 00:00:31,370
The strange thing is,
we built them. Again and again.

11
00:00:31,995 --> 00:00:33,995
So why doesn't anyone use them?

12
00:00:36,335 --> 00:00:39,375
Building a flying car
was never the hard part.

13
00:00:39,973 --> 00:00:43,173
The hard part starts
the moment you have more than one.

14
00:00:44,159 --> 00:00:45,979
But let's start with one.

15
00:00:47,137 --> 00:00:50,057
A car and a plane
want almost opposite things.

16
00:00:50,795 --> 00:00:54,235
A car has to be strong and heavy enough
to survive a crash.

17
00:00:54,635 --> 00:00:57,195
A plane has to be as light as possible.

18
00:00:57,933 --> 00:00:59,873
A car has to fit in a lane.

19
00:01:00,493 --> 00:01:02,893
A plane needs wings wider than the road.

20
00:01:03,599 --> 00:01:05,919
So every flying car is a compromise.

21
00:01:06,779 --> 00:01:09,639
The wings fold, or come off,
or get left behind.

22
00:01:10,460 --> 00:01:12,120
And it still needs a runway.

23
00:01:12,920 --> 00:01:14,800
The Dutch flying car that's road-legal

24
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:16,640
needs a hundred and eighty metres of it

25
00:01:16,870 --> 00:01:18,080
just to take off.

26
00:01:18,686 --> 00:01:21,706
So to fly to work,
you'd first drive to an airfield.

27
00:01:23,446 --> 00:01:28,746
In 1949, the aircraft maker William Piper
put it in one sentence.

28
00:01:29,215 --> 00:01:33,475
The roadable plane, he said, is neither
a good plane nor a good automobile.

29
00:01:35,985 --> 00:01:39,945
Two years earlier, an American company
had tried to get around that.

30
00:01:40,519 --> 00:01:42,839
Its flying car came in two halves.

31
00:01:43,559 --> 00:01:46,079
You'd buy the car,
and rent the wing at the airport.

32
00:01:46,889 --> 00:01:50,089
It flew for the first time
in November 1947.

33
00:01:50,902 --> 00:01:53,562
Three days later, it ran low on fuel

34
00:01:53,642 --> 00:01:57,202
and had to make a forced landing
on mud flats near San Diego.

35
00:01:58,067 --> 00:02:02,847
The story goes that the pilot had checked
the wrong fuel gauge, the one for the car.

36
00:02:03,561 --> 00:02:06,181
Whether or not that's true,
it shows the problem.

37
00:02:06,801 --> 00:02:10,341
Two machines in one body
means two of everything to get right.

38
00:02:12,901 --> 00:02:17,021
So engineers had a better idea.
Skip the runway.

39
00:02:17,461 --> 00:02:19,901
Take off straight up,
from a street, or a roof.

40
00:02:20,574 --> 00:02:23,414
That's what most flying cars today
really are.

41
00:02:24,074 --> 00:02:27,574
Not cars with wings,
but small aircraft that rise vertically.

42
00:02:28,331 --> 00:02:30,571
And that's where physics sends the bill.

43
00:02:31,064 --> 00:02:33,044
At a hundred kilometres an hour,

44
00:02:33,124 --> 00:02:36,324
an ordinary car needs
about thirteen kilowatts.

45
00:02:36,613 --> 00:02:38,653
Its weight is carried by the road.

46
00:02:39,293 --> 00:02:41,573
The engine only has to keep it moving.

47
00:02:42,343 --> 00:02:45,143
A machine that hovers
has to carry all of that weight itself,

48
00:02:45,373 --> 00:02:48,623
by throwing air downwards, every second.

49
00:02:49,266 --> 00:02:52,186
For something the size of a car,
that takes somewhere between

50
00:02:52,416 --> 00:02:54,786
twenty and forty times as much power.

51
00:02:55,485 --> 00:02:58,265
And the smaller the rotors,
the worse it gets.

52
00:02:58,794 --> 00:03:03,294
A helicopter does the same job
with about ten times the power of a car.

53
00:03:03,582 --> 00:03:06,602
But only because its rotor
is ten metres across.

54
00:03:07,147 --> 00:03:10,847
So it's efficient exactly
because it could never fit on a road.

55
00:03:11,531 --> 00:03:15,251
To keep a car in the air cheaply,
we'd need something completely new.

56
00:03:15,631 --> 00:03:17,711
Something like antigravity.

57
00:03:18,471 --> 00:03:21,311
And that's far beyond anything
we know how to build.

58
00:03:22,991 --> 00:03:26,471
And if the power comes from batteries,
there's a second problem.

59
00:03:26,941 --> 00:03:30,841
A kilogram of petrol holds
about fifty times as much energy

60
00:03:31,041 --> 00:03:33,321
as a kilogram of today's battery packs.

61
00:03:34,077 --> 00:03:38,037
Electric motors waste much less of it,
but even then, the battery

62
00:03:38,267 --> 00:03:41,077
still delivers only about
a fifteenth as much.

63
00:03:41,789 --> 00:03:44,509
A fuel tank gets lighter as it empties.

64
00:03:44,589 --> 00:03:46,149
A battery doesn't.

65
00:03:48,263 --> 00:03:51,803
Then there's the question
the old illustrations never asked.

66
00:03:52,255 --> 00:03:53,735
Who's flying it?

67
00:03:54,698 --> 00:03:56,278
A private pilot's licence takes

68
00:03:56,508 --> 00:04:00,458
at least forty hours in the air,
and in practice usually more.

69
00:04:01,045 --> 00:04:02,825
In the United States, there are

70
00:04:03,055 --> 00:04:04,785
around fourteen hundred licensed drivers

71
00:04:05,015 --> 00:04:06,785
for every private pilot.

72
00:04:07,498 --> 00:04:11,218
So there are really only three ways
to give everyone a flying car.

73
00:04:11,597 --> 00:04:13,477
Everyone learns to fly.

74
00:04:13,985 --> 00:04:16,505
Someone on the ground flies it for them.

75
00:04:16,889 --> 00:04:18,469
Or it flies itself.

76
00:04:19,883 --> 00:04:21,983
The second one runs out of people.

77
00:04:22,683 --> 00:04:25,083
In a NASA simulation,
one person on the ground

78
00:04:25,313 --> 00:04:27,583
could safely look after three aircraft.

79
00:04:28,586 --> 00:04:30,126
Which leaves the third.

80
00:04:30,683 --> 00:04:35,523
In China, a pilotless air taxi
has been certified since 2023.

81
00:04:36,069 --> 00:04:39,709
Even so, it hasn't carried
a single paying passenger yet.

82
00:04:40,368 --> 00:04:43,248
So the flying car may need more
than better engines.

83
00:04:44,148 --> 00:04:47,388
It needs software we'd trust
with the sky above our heads.

84
00:04:47,861 --> 00:04:50,681
And so far, we've only talked
about one machine.

85
00:04:52,013 --> 00:04:54,753
Now put a few thousand of them
over one city.

86
00:04:56,058 --> 00:04:58,458
When a car breaks down, you pull over.

87
00:04:58,798 --> 00:05:01,318
In the air, there's nowhere to pull over.

88
00:05:01,745 --> 00:05:04,085
Antigravity would help here too.

89
00:05:04,445 --> 00:05:07,225
You could pull over onto a hard shoulder
in the sky.

90
00:05:07,765 --> 00:05:09,905
You just couldn't get out and walk.

91
00:05:11,035 --> 00:05:15,755
Per hour of travel, private flying is
about fifty times deadlier than driving.

92
00:05:16,264 --> 00:05:20,144
And over a city, it isn't only
the people on board who are at risk.

93
00:05:20,440 --> 00:05:23,720
It's everyone underneath,
who never chose to fly at all.

94
00:05:26,030 --> 00:05:27,630
Then there's the noise.

95
00:05:28,239 --> 00:05:31,779
The newest air taxis
really are much quieter than helicopters.

96
00:05:32,465 --> 00:05:34,025
But even a quiet aircraft,

97
00:05:34,105 --> 00:05:36,485
times a thousand,
over the street where you live,

98
00:05:36,565 --> 00:05:38,425
every morning and every evening.

99
00:05:40,455 --> 00:05:43,015
And every one of them
has to land somewhere.

100
00:05:43,530 --> 00:05:47,270
São Paulo has one of the biggest
helicopter fleets of any city,

101
00:05:47,550 --> 00:05:52,210
with about four hundred helicopters
and around two hundred and sixty helipads.

102
00:05:52,621 --> 00:05:55,221
That's what it takes
for just a few hundred machines.

103
00:05:57,931 --> 00:06:00,791
So now, the last step. Make it millions.

104
00:06:02,140 --> 00:06:05,140
London has more than
two and a half million cars.

105
00:06:05,620 --> 00:06:08,400
If just one in a hundred
became a flying car,

106
00:06:08,620 --> 00:06:11,860
that's over twenty-six thousand aircraft
over one city.

107
00:06:12,343 --> 00:06:16,923
That's about as many as are in the air
over the entire planet at any one moment.

108
00:06:18,116 --> 00:06:20,796
At its busiest, the whole of
the United States handles

109
00:06:21,026 --> 00:06:25,016
about five and a half thousand flights
in the air at the same time.

110
00:06:25,372 --> 00:06:28,932
It takes fourteen thousand
air traffic controllers to do it.

111
00:06:29,257 --> 00:06:32,677
And every one of those flights
has an assigned route and altitude,

112
00:06:32,957 --> 00:06:34,857
and a plan filed in advance.

113
00:06:35,468 --> 00:06:40,028
A city full of flying cars would need all
of that, for trips to the supermarket.

114
00:06:42,908 --> 00:06:44,788
So that's the real answer.

115
00:06:45,659 --> 00:06:49,279
A flying car is a perfectly good idea
for one person.

116
00:06:49,915 --> 00:06:52,315
It just stops working
when everyone has one.

117
00:06:55,369 --> 00:06:57,829
Which is why the biggest companies
building them today

118
00:06:58,059 --> 00:06:59,949
have quietly changed the plan.

119
00:07:00,427 --> 00:07:02,427
They're not selling flying cars.

120
00:07:02,987 --> 00:07:04,627
They're building air taxis.

121
00:07:05,189 --> 00:07:08,029
A small number of aircraft,
flown by professional pilots,

122
00:07:08,259 --> 00:07:10,889
on a few carefully chosen routes.

123
00:07:11,757 --> 00:07:14,437
In Dubai, Joby plans
to fly passengers between

124
00:07:14,667 --> 00:07:17,637
four landing sites,
one of them at the airport.

125
00:07:18,467 --> 00:07:20,847
But even this smaller dream is hard.

126
00:07:21,307 --> 00:07:25,887
Two of Europe's best-known
air taxi companies, Lilium and Volocopter,

127
00:07:26,007 --> 00:07:29,207
filed for insolvency
within two months of each other.

128
00:07:29,565 --> 00:07:34,605
And Airbus paused its own project, saying
battery technology wasn't ready yet.

129
00:07:34,965 --> 00:07:39,585
So far, nowhere in the world is an air
taxi like this carrying paying passengers.

130
00:07:42,535 --> 00:07:43,775
If they do take off,

131
00:07:43,855 --> 00:07:46,095
they'll look less like the future
we were promised,

132
00:07:46,175 --> 00:07:48,575
and more like something we already have.

133
00:07:48,881 --> 00:07:50,441
A helicopter service.

134
00:07:50,861 --> 00:07:52,781
Just quieter, and maybe cheaper.

135
00:07:55,271 --> 00:07:58,391
Maybe that's where the flying car
belonged all along.

136
00:07:59,393 --> 00:08:02,173
We imagined it
as a replacement for the car.

137
00:08:02,660 --> 00:08:05,660
But its real place
may be somewhere else entirely.

138
00:08:06,383 --> 00:08:09,303
Not in every garage,
but on a handful of routes,

139
00:08:09,403 --> 00:08:12,443
for the trips where flying
really is worth the cost.

140
00:08:13,321 --> 00:08:15,541
We didn't fail to build flying cars.

141
00:08:16,161 --> 00:08:18,801
We built them,
and found out what they're good for.

142
00:08:21,121 --> 00:08:24,521
So, would you actually trust
a flying car above your city?

143
00:08:25,201 --> 00:08:26,941
And if not, what would it take?

144
00:08:27,641 --> 00:08:29,091
Let me know in the comments.
